    "agent_last_message": "The tool is in `output/` and builds with `cmake -B build && cmake --build build`, producing `build/wordcount`. It uses only the C++20 standard library, and the build defaults to an optimized Release build. It also builds cleanly from a fresh copy of the directory, and I removed my own build directory so a stale CMake cache can't break yours.\n\n## Choices the spec left open\n\nFour behaviors weren't pinned down. These are the ones to check if your expectations differ; each is a small change:\n\n1. **Non-UTF-8 input is \"malformed\" (exit 1).** Binary or Latin-1 files are rejected, and the error message gives the byte offset of the first bad sequence. The tool can't write such words into valid JSON without guessing their encoding.\n2. **Lowercasing covers all of Unicode, not just A\u2013Z.** The spec says output words are \"in lowercase\", and ASCII-only folding would print `\u00c9COLE` as `\u00c9cole`. It matches Python's `str.lower()` exactly, including the Greek final-sigma rule.\n3. **`--help` exits with 1.** Your exit codes say 0 means the output was written, so `--help` prints usage and exits 1 like any other invalid invocation.\n4. **Output problems:** an output path that can't be created exits 1, and a failure while writing (for example a full disk) exits 2. The output file is only created after the analysis succeeds, so a failed run never wipes an existing report.\n\nEverything else follows the spec literally: characters are bytes, lines are counted by `\\n`, only the six listed whitespace characters split words, and ties are broken alphabetically.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Unit tests:** 80,650 checks, all passing, including under AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and ThreadSanitizer. They compare results for every block size down to 1 byte and several thread counts against a simple single-pass version.\n- **End-to-end tests:** 671 checks run the real program against an independent Python model of the spec. They cover every exit-code case and random inputs up to 9 MB.\n- **Exhaustive chec",
